var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#Shakti.jl","page":"Shakti.jl","text":"A Julia solver for the SHAKTI subglacial hydrology model (Sommers and others, 2018): a continuum model of hydraulic head, effective pressure, water flux, and drainage-system geometry (gap height) beneath an ice sheet, that transitions smoothly between laminar (distributed) and turbulent (channelized) flow regimes rather than treating them as separate model components.\n\nShakti solves the nonlinear elliptic equation for hydraulic head via Picard iteration each timestep, then evolves the gap height explicitly or implicitly, using either a direct (sparse Cholesky) or iterative (preconditioned conjugate gradient, with Chebyshev or algebraic-multigrid preconditioning) linear solver. It runs on CPU (Threads) or GPU (CUDA/Metal) backends via ParallelStencil.jl, selected once at load time through Preferences-backed backend/floattype constants rather than a runtime argument, so every kernel compiles for the right array/element type.\n\nSee the API page for the full reference, or the README for installation and a quick-start example.","title":"Shakti.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-structure","page":"Shakti.jl","text":"The package is organized around a handful of core types, each with an abstract supertype where there's more than one way to do that piece of the physics/numerics:\n\nAbstraction Purpose Concrete implementation(s)\nGrid Regular Cartesian grid geometry Grid\nState Every hydrology field: hydraulic head, water/overburden/effective pressure, gap height, melt rate, water flux, Reynolds number, transmissivity, ... State\nModelParameters Physical constants (densities, viscosity, Glen's-law exponent, bed-bump geometry, ...) ModelParameters\nAbstractSlidingLaw How basal shear stress taub (feeding frictional melt) is obtained RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw, LinearSlidingLaw, PrescribedSlidingLaw\nAbstractMeltInput Englacial-to-bed meltwater input (moulins/crevasses) ConstantMeltInput, SeasonalMeltInput\nAbstractLinearSystem How the linearized elliptic equation is represented SparseAssembledLinearSystem, MatrixFreeLinearSystem\nAbstractLinearSolver How that system is solved each Picard iteration CholeskyDirectSolver (direct), CGIterativeSolver (iterative, with AMGPreconditioner/ChebyshevPreconditioner)\nAbstractHeadScheme / AbstractGapScheme Time-integration scheme for head / gap height EllipticHeadScheme (Picard); ExplicitGapScheme, ImplicitGapScheme\nAbstractObserver How output is recorded NoObserver, LiveObserver (in-memory), IOObserver (to disk: NetCDF/HDF5/JLD2/CSV)\nSimulation Bundles everything above and drives the time loop Simulation, run!","title":"Package structure"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Backend-selection","page":"API","text":"","title":"Backend selection"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Grid","page":"API","text":"","title":"Grid"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#State","page":"API","text":"","title":"State"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Model-parameters","page":"API","text":"","title":"Model parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Mask","page":"API","text":"","title":"Mask"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Melt-input","page":"API","text":"","title":"Melt input"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#K-face-scheme","page":"API","text":"","title":"K-face scheme"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Sliding-laws-and-melt-rate","page":"API","text":"","title":"Sliding laws and melt rate"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Linear-solvers","page":"API","text":"","title":"Linear solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Preconditioners","page":"API","text":"","title":"Preconditioners"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Elliptic-(Picard)-solver","page":"API","text":"","title":"Elliptic (Picard) solver"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Simulation","page":"API","text":"","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Derived-fields","page":"API","text":"","title":"Derived fields"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Water-flux,-Reynolds-number,-transmissivity","page":"API","text":"","title":"Water flux, Reynolds number, transmissivity"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Gap-height","page":"API","text":"","title":"Gap height"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Initial-conditions","page":"API","text":"","title":"Initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Checkpoint-/-restart","page":"API","text":"","title":"Checkpoint / restart"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Running-a-simulation","page":"API","text":"","title":"Running a simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Observers-(output-recording)","page":"API","text":"","title":"Observers (output recording)"},{"category":"section","location":"API/#Animation","page":"API","text":"","title":"Animation"},{"category":"constant","location":"API/#Shakti.backend","page":"API","text":"Which array/compute backend Shakti runs on: \"Threads\" (CPU), \"CUDA\", or \"Metal\". A Preferences-backed constant, read once at module load from LocalPreferences.toml (default \"Threads\") rather than a runtime argument – see Preferences.jl for how to set it before using Shakti.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.backend"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.floattype","page":"API","text":"The floating-point element type every State/Grid/ModelParameters field is stored as (Float64 or Float32). A Preferences-backed constant, read once at module load from LocalPreferences.toml (default Float64) rather than a runtime argument – see Preferences.jl for how to set it before using Shakti.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.floattype"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.Grid","page":"API","text":"A regular Cartesian grid: nx * ny cells spanning a domain of size lx * ly, with cell-center coordinate vectors x/y starting at the origin. dx2/dy2 are dx^2/dy^2, precomputed once since they appear in every finite-difference/finite-volume stencil in the solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.Grid"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.State","page":"API","text":"Every field of the subglacial hydrology solve: cell-centered quantities (hydraulic head, water and overburden pressure, effective pressure, gap height, melt rate and its three heat-source components, ...) and the x-/y-face quantities needed for the finite-volume flux/gradient stencils (Nx+1 x Ny and Nx x Ny+1 respectively). Every field shares one array/element type A, so switching backend or floating-point precision (see the Preferences-backed backend/floattype constants in Shakti.jl) only ever touches Grid/State construction, not the kernels that operate on them. Build one with State(grid), then populate it via set_initial_conditions!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.State"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.ModelParameters","page":"API","text":"Physical constants and model parameters shared by every kernel, plus three precomputed \"canonical\" exponents (n_exp, n_minus_1_exp, inv_n_exp) used for fast exponentiation of Glen's-flow-law-style powers – see the module-level note below the constructor for why. Build one with the keyword constructor below rather than this positional one directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.ModelParameters"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.pow","page":"API","text":"pow(x, n::Integer) -> Any\n\n\nx^n, dispatching on n's type: n::Integer takes Julia's fast power-by-squaring path; n::AbstractFloat falls back to the general (much slower) floating-point-exponent path. Meant to be called with an exponent that has already been through canonical_exponent – see the module-level note above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.pow"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.canonical_exponent","page":"API","text":"canonical_exponent(n::AbstractFloat) -> Any\n\n\nReturns n converted to a plain Int if it has an exact integer value (e.g. 3.0 -> 3), otherwise returns n unchanged. See the module-level note above for why/how this is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.canonical_exponent"},{"category":"constant","location":"API/#Shakti.GROUNDED","page":"API","text":"Mask value: dynamic hydrology solved here (Picard/Poisson elliptic solve + gap-height evolution).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.GROUNDED"},{"category":"constant","location":"API/#Shakti.OCEAN","page":"API","text":"Mask value: Dirichlet boundary, pw = p_atm - rho_w*g*min(zb, 0) (zb = bedrock elevation relative to sea level, positive up, so a marine bed at zb < 0 gets the correct hydrostatic pressure at depth -zb).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.OCEAN"},{"category":"constant","location":"API/#Shakti.LAND","page":"API","text":"Mask value: Dirichlet boundary, pw = p_atm (0.0 by default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.LAND"},{"category":"constant","location":"API/#Shakti.OTHER_BASIN","page":"API","text":"Mask value: not solved here; frozen row. Any GROUNDED neighbour treats the shared face as zero-flux (Neumann), and any face-based quantity (dhdx, dpwdx, q_x, ...) touching this cell is zeroed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.OTHER_BASIN"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_face_masks!","page":"API","text":"compute_face_masks!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nRecomputes s.valid_x/s.valid_y from s.mask. Must be called (directly, or via set_initial_conditions!) any time s.mask changes.\n\nNotes\n\nA face is invalid iff either cell it connects is OTHER_BASIN: that cell's hydrology isn't solved, so any gradient computed across that face would spuriously reflect a frozen, non-evolving neighbour value rather than a real head/pressure difference. LAND and OCEAN faces are left valid, since those are genuine (Dirichlet) drainage boundaries where a real flux is physically meaningful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_face_masks!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.apply_mask_to_sliding!","page":"API","text":"apply_mask_to_sliding!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nZeroes s.ub_x/s.ub_y on any face touching a non-GROUNDED cell: sliding velocity is only physically meaningful where grounded ice is actually sliding on a bed with an evolving hydraulic system, so a face touching OTHER_BASIN (or a domain boundary) cannot contribute melting via the taub . ub term, consistent with the Neumann condition imposed on the elliptic head solve there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.apply_mask_to_sliding!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractMeltInput","page":"API","text":"How s.ieb (englacial-to-bed meltwater input, e.g. moulins/crevasses) is set up and evolved over time – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype picks a fixed field (ConstantMeltInput) or a time-varying seasonal cycle (SeasonalMeltInput). Every subtype implements initialize_ieb! (called once, during set_initial_conditions!), compute_ieb! (a single-cell lookup, called from inside linear_solver.jl's @parallel assembly kernels), and update_ieb! (called once per real timestep, outside the Picard loop, so s.ieb stays fixed across every Picard iteration within that timestep).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractMeltInput"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.ConstantMeltInput","page":"API","text":"A time-independent ieb field: whatever was passed to initialize_ieb! is used unchanged for the whole run (update_ieb! is a no-op).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.ConstantMeltInput"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.SeasonalMeltInput","page":"API","text":"Reproduces the seasonal-cycle experiment from the original SHAKTI paper (Sommers et al. 2018, Sect. 3.3): i_e->b is applied uniformly over the whole domain, held at a winter baseline (i_min) except during a cosine-shaped melt-season window [t_start, t_start+period] (year fraction), where it swings up to a summer peak and back down to i_min at both ends of the window – continuously, since the paper's default amplitude/offset (492.75/493.75) make the cosine's boundary value exactly i_min.\n\nNotes\n\nHolds only scalar fields (no arrays): like ConstantMeltInput/Arithmetic/ Harmonic, this struct is passed by value into linear_solver.jl's @parallel kernels (for compute_ieb!'s dispatch), which requires kernel arguments to be bitstypes – an array field would break that. omega (2*pi/period) is precomputed once at construction rather than recomputed every update_ieb! call, same idiom as model_parameters.jl's canonical_exponent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.SeasonalMeltInput"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.initialize_ieb!","page":"API","text":"initialize_ieb!(\n    _::ConstantMeltInput,\n    state::State,\n    ieb::AbstractArray\n) -> Any\n\n\nSeeds state.ieb from ieb. Called once, during set_initial_conditions!.\n\n\n\n\n\ninitialize_ieb!(\n    _::SeasonalMeltInput,\n    state::State,\n    ieb::AbstractArray\n) -> Any\n\n\nSeeds state.ieb from ieb. Called once, during set_initial_conditions!; the actual seasonal cycle is applied later, per timestep, by update_ieb!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.initialize_ieb!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_ieb!","page":"API","text":"compute_ieb!(\n    _::ConstantMeltInput,\n    ieb::AbstractArray,\n    i::Int64,\n    j::Int64\n) -> Any\n\n\nSingle-cell lookup of the current ieb value at (i, j), used inside linear_solver.jl's @parallel assembly kernels (which need a bitstype-safe way to read melt input without carrying a whole AbstractMeltInput struct into the kernel).\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_ieb!(\n    _::SeasonalMeltInput,\n    ieb::AbstractArray,\n    i::Int64,\n    j::Int64\n) -> Any\n\n\nSingle-cell lookup of the current ieb value at (i, j) (already up to date for this timestep, via update_ieb!), used inside linear_solver.jl's @parallel assembly kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_ieb!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.update_ieb!","page":"API","text":"update_ieb!(_::ConstantMeltInput, state::State, t) -> State\n\n\nNo-op: ieb never changes after initialize_ieb! under ConstantMeltInput, so there's nothing to do per timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate_ieb!(mi::SeasonalMeltInput, state::State, t) -> State\n\n\nUpdates state.ieb (uniformly over the whole domain) for the current simulation time t (elapsed seconds, see run.jl's total_time), following mi's cosine-shaped seasonal cycle.\n\nNotes\n\nCalled once per real timestep, outside the Picard loop, so state.ieb stays fixed across every Picard iteration within that timestep – same as ConstantMeltInput's ieb being fixed for the whole run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.update_ieb!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractKFaceScheme","page":"API","text":"How hydraulic conductivity K is combined across two neighbouring GROUNDED cells' shared face into a single face conductance – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype picks arithmetic vs. harmonic averaging (see compute_K_face and boundary_K_face).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractKFaceScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.Arithmetic","page":"API","text":"Arithmetic-mean face conductance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.Arithmetic"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.Harmonic","page":"API","text":"Harmonic-mean face conductance: penalizes a face where either neighbour has low conductivity more strongly than the arithmetic mean does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.Harmonic"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_K_face","page":"API","text":"compute_K_face(_::Arithmetic, K, i1, j1, i2, j2) -> Any\n\n\nFace conductance between cells (i1,j1) and (i2,j2), (K[i1,j1] + K[i2,j2]) / 2 under Arithmetic or 2*K[i1,j1]*K[i2,j2] / (K[i1,j1] + K[i2,j2] + eps) under Harmonic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_K_face"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractSensibleHeatScheme","page":"API","text":"Whether compute_mdot! includes the sensible-heat term – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype (WithSensibleHeat/NoSensibleHeat), decided ONCE in Simulation's constructor (from p.ct/p.cw, see simulation.jl) rather than every call, so turning the term off skips compute_sensible!'s kernel launch and array traffic entirely instead of just multiplying its contribution by zero – same \"dispatch on a type decided once outside the hot loop\" idiom as canonical_exponent/pow (model_parameters.jl) and the head/gap schemes (simulation.jl).\n\nNotes\n\nDefined here, next to their sole consumer (compute_mdot! below), rather than in simulation.jl: elliptic_solver.jl (included before simulation.jl for its own PicardSolver/Simulation ordering reasons) needs AbstractSensibleHeatScheme to type-annotate shs, so this file is included before elliptic_solver.jl too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractSensibleHeatScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.WithSensibleHeat","page":"API","text":"Include the sensible-heat term (ct*cw*rho_w*sens) in compute_mdot!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.WithSensibleHeat"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.NoSensibleHeat","page":"API","text":"Skip the sensible-heat term in compute_mdot! entirely (not just multiply by zero).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.NoSensibleHeat"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractSlidingLaw","page":"API","text":"How taub (basal shear stress, feeding compute_mdot!'s frictional-heating term) is obtained – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype (RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw, LinearSlidingLaw, PrescribedSlidingLaw), decided once per Simulation (same idiom as AbstractSensibleHeatScheme above).\n\nNotes\n\nThe original SHAKTI paper (Sommers et al. 2018) never actually specifies this – taub is listed in its Table 1 as a state variable with no formula, since it normally comes from a coupled ice-dynamics solve; Table 2's constants have no friction coefficient at all. So this is a real modeling choice, not just an internal solver detail; keeping it explicit and swappable rather than silently baking in one assumption is the point of this abstraction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractSlidingLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw","page":"API","text":"Regularized-Coulomb sliding law: taub -> C*N as ub/N^n*lambda -> infinity (hard Coulomb limit), but stays finite (unlike a bare Coulomb law) as ub -> 0. Recomputed from N/ub every Picard iteration (see compute_taub_x! etc. below).\n\nNotes\n\nn/inv_n are read from p.n_exp/p.inv_n_exp, canonicalized once at ModelParameters construction (see model_parameters.jl's pow/canonical_exponent note), not per call or per grid cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.PrescribedSlidingLaw","page":"API","text":"taub_x/taub_y prescribed once (initialize_taub!, from whatever formula or data the caller supplies – e.g. a driving-stress balance taub = rho_i*g*H*(surface slope), the standard parameter-free choice for synthetic slab/margin setups) and left unchanged thereafter: compute_taub_x! etc. are no-ops for this law.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.PrescribedSlidingLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.LinearSlidingLaw","page":"API","text":"LinearSlidingLaw(g::Grid, C) -> LinearSlidingLaw\n\n\nBuilds a LinearSlidingLaw on grid g from a drag coefficient C given at cell centers (an (nx, ny) array, e.g. an inverted per-cell field) or as a uniform scalar, staggering it onto faces and squaring it once here rather than in the per-iteration hot path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.LinearSlidingLaw"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.initialize_taub!","page":"API","text":"initialize_taub!(\n    _::RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw,\n    state::State,\n    taub_x::AbstractArray,\n    taub_y::AbstractArray\n) -> State\n\n\nSets up state.taub_x/state.taub_y for sl before the Picard loop starts. A no-op for RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw/LinearSlidingLaw (taub is instead recomputed every Picard iteration from N/ub, see compute_taub_x! etc. below); copies taub_x/ taub_y into state once for PrescribedSlidingLaw (see its own docstring).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.initialize_taub!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_taub_x!","page":"API","text":"compute_taub_x!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    _::PrescribedSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nNo-op for PrescribedSlidingLaw: taub_x/taub_y were already set once by initialize_taub! and never change.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_taub_x!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    sl::RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.taub_x under RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw from the current s.N/s.ub_x.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_taub_x!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    sl::LinearSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.taub_x under LinearSlidingLaw from the current s.N/s.ub_x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_taub_x!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_taub_y!","page":"API","text":"compute_taub_y!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    _::PrescribedSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nNo-op for PrescribedSlidingLaw, the y-face counterpart of the compute_taub_x! method above.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_taub_y!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    sl::RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.taub_y under RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw, the y-face counterpart of the compute_taub_x! method above.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_taub_y!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    sl::LinearSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.taub_y under LinearSlidingLaw, the y-face counterpart of the compute_taub_x! method above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_taub_y!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_taub_xy!","page":"API","text":"compute_taub_xy!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    _::PrescribedSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nNo-op for PrescribedSlidingLaw, the fused compute_taub_x! + compute_taub_y! counterpart above.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_taub_xy!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    sl::RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_taub_x! + compute_taub_y! under RegularizedCoulombSlidingLaw: one @parallel launch instead of two.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_taub_xy!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    sl::LinearSlidingLaw\n) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_taub_x! + compute_taub_y! under LinearSlidingLaw: one @parallel launch instead of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_taub_xy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_shear!","page":"API","text":"compute_shear!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.shear: the frictional (sliding) heating contribution to the melt rate, |u_b . taub| averaged from faces onto cell centers. Standalone/diagnostic use (e.g. as a tracked_obs name, see Simulation's tracked_obs) – compute_mdot!'s hot path recomputes this term itself in a fused kernel rather than calling this function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_shear!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_potential!","page":"API","text":"compute_potential!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.potential: the potential-energy-dissipation contribution to the melt rate (water flowing down the hydraulic-head gradient), |q . dhdx| averaged from faces onto cell centers. Standalone/diagnostic use, same caveat as compute_shear!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_potential!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_sensible!","page":"API","text":"compute_sensible!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.sensible: the sensible-heat-exchange contribution to the melt rate (water moving to regions of different pressure-melting-point temperature), q . dpwdx averaged from faces onto cell centers. Requires s.dpwdx/s.dpwdy already current. Standalone/diagnostic use, same caveat as compute_shear!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_sensible!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_mdot!","page":"API","text":"compute_mdot!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    _::WithSensibleHeat\n) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.mdot (subglacial melt rate) = geothermal flux + frictional (sliding) heating + potential energy released by water flowing downgradient + sensible heat exchanged as water moves to regions of different pressure melting point, all divided by the latent heat of fusion p.L. Also refreshes s.shear/s.potential/s.sensible as a side effect (needed by compute_shear! etc. for standalone/diagnostic use), computed via its own fused kernel rather than by calling those three functions (one launch instead of four).\n\nDispatches on sim.shs (decided once in Simulation's constructor from p.ct/p.cw, see AbstractSensibleHeatScheme): the NoSensibleHeat method never touches s.sensible or s.dpwdx/s.dpwdy at all, rather than computing sensible and multiplying by a zero ct*cw prefactor.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_mdot!(\n    s::State,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    _::NoSensibleHeat\n) -> State\n\n\ncompute_mdot! without the sensible-heat term – see the WithSensibleHeat method's docstring above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_mdot!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractLinearSolver","page":"API","text":"How the linearized elliptic equation for h (one per Picard iteration) is solved – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype picks a direct (AbstractDirectSolver) or iterative (AbstractIterativeSolver) method, via solve_linear_system!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractLinearSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractDirectSolver","page":"API","text":"A direct linear solver (currently just CholeskyDirectSolver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractDirectSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractIterativeSolver","page":"API","text":"An iterative linear solver (currently just CGIterativeSolver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractIterativeSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractLinearSystem","page":"API","text":"How the assembled linear system is represented – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype picks between an explicit sparse matrix (SparseAssembledLinearSystem, CPU-only, needed by CholeskyDirectSolver) and a matrix-free stencil representation (MatrixFreeLinearSystem, GPU-capable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractLinearSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.SparseAssembledLinearSystem","page":"API","text":"The linear system as an explicit sparse matrix M (5-point stencil) and dense rhs, plus index arrays (idxP/idxE/idxW/idxN/idxS) mapping each grid cell to where its diagonal/neighbour coefficients live in M.nzval – built once (the sparsity pattern never changes across Picard iterations/timesteps) so update_SALS! only ever rewrites values, not structure. CPU-only (SparseArrays/CHOLMOD have no GPU path); see MatrixFreeLinearSystem for the GPU-capable alternative.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.SparseAssembledLinearSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.MatrixFreeLinearSystem","page":"API","text":"The linear system as per-cell stencil coefficients (aP/aE/aW/aN/aS, one value per grid cell each) with no sparse matrix ever materialized – the matvec (stencil_matvec_kernel! via StencilOperator) applies the stencil directly. GPU-capable (every array lands on the active backend, see the constructor below); the alternative to SparseAssembledLinearSystem for backends without a sparse-direct-solver path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.MatrixFreeLinearSystem"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.CholeskyDirectSolver","page":"API","text":"Direct solve via sparse Cholesky factorization (SparseArrays/CHOLMOD) of the assembled SparseAssembledLinearSystem – the fastest solver at every grid size benchmarked so far (see test/benchmarks/), but CPU-only. Refactorizes in place each solve, reusing the symbolic factorization since the sparsity pattern never changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.CholeskyDirectSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.CGIterativeSolver","page":"API","text":"Iterative solve via preconditioned conjugate gradient (Krylov.jl's cg!) – valid because the assembled operator is symmetric positive definite (diffusion with reciprocal face fluxes plus a strictly positive diagonal reaction term from the Newton-linearized creep closure, Dirichlet neighbours eliminated symmetrically). Works over either linear-system representation (SparseAssembledLinearSystem or MatrixFreeLinearSystem, chosen via the LSy type parameter at construction, see the constructors below) and warm-starts from the previous head each solve.\n\nNotes\n\nprecond picks the preconditioner: nothing for plain Jacobi, or an AMGPreconditioner/ ChebyshevPreconditioner (see preconditioner.jl) for a much faster-converging accelerated preconditioner – AMG is the default for SparseAssembledLinearSystem (near mesh-independent CG iteration counts; measured to consistently beat both plain Jacobi and Chebyshev, see test/benchmarks/) but is CPU/SparseMatrixCSC-only, so MatrixFreeLinearSystem defaults to plain Jacobi with Chebyshev available as a GPU-capable accelerated option instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.CGIterativeSolver"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.solve_linear_system!","page":"API","text":"solve_linear_system!(\n    ls::CholeskyDirectSolver,\n    s::State,\n    g::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    kfs::AbstractKFaceScheme,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput\n) -> Any\n\n\nSolves the linearized elliptic equation for h, storing the result in s.h – the CholeskyDirectSolver method: rebuilds the system (update_SALS!), refactorizes in place, and solves via ldiv!.\n\n\n\n\n\nsolve_linear_system!(\n    ls::CGIterativeSolver{<:SparseAssembledLinearSystem},\n    s::State,\n    g::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    kfs::AbstractKFaceScheme,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput\n) -> Any\n\n\nSolves the linearized elliptic equation for h, storing the result in s.h – the CGIterativeSolver over SparseAssembledLinearSystem method: rebuilds the system, refreshes the preconditioner, and runs cg! warm-started from the current s.h.\n\n\n\n\n\nsolve_linear_system!(\n    ls::CGIterativeSolver{<:MatrixFreeLinearSystem},\n    s::State,\n    g::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    kfs::AbstractKFaceScheme,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput\n) -> Any\n\n\nSolves the linearized elliptic equation for h, storing the result in s.h – the CGIterativeSolver over MatrixFreeLinearSystem method, using StencilOperator as cg!'s matvec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.solve_linear_system!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.ChebyshevPreconditioner","page":"API","text":"A GPU-capable, dot-product-free accelerated preconditioner for CGIterativeSolver: approximates A^-1 by a degree-degree Chebyshev polynomial in the Jacobi-scaled operator (Saad Algorithm 12.1), applied via LinearAlgebra.ldiv! below using only matvecs and axpy-style updates – no reductions (and so no GPU->CPU syncs) within one preconditioner application. lambda_min/lambda_max (the polynomial's eigenvalue bounds) are refreshed once per solve by update_chebyshev_bounds!, not on every ldiv! call.\n\nNotes\n\nConfirmed on real GPU hardware (see the module-level note above) that this does NOT eliminate CG's own per-iteration host syncs (those come from CG's own dot products, not the preconditioner) – measured to lose to plain Jacobi in wall time at the grid sizes tested. Kept available as an option (and the only GPU-capable accelerated one) since a differently-shaped or larger problem could tip that balance; AMGPreconditioner is the better choice when available (CPU/SparseMatrixCSC-only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.ChebyshevPreconditioner"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.update_chebyshev_bounds!","page":"API","text":"update_chebyshev_bounds!(\n    P::ChebyshevPreconditioner,\n    rhs::AbstractVector\n) -> ChebyshevPreconditioner\n\n\nRefreshes P.lambda_min/P.lambda_max from the current rhs via estimate_eigenvalue_bounds – cheap (P.nsteps_estimate matvecs) but real, since the operator's spectrum shifts between Picard iterations/timesteps. Falls back to the previous (valid) bounds if the estimate is numerically invalid (see the extensive in-code note for why that can happen and why the fallback is always safe).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.update_chebyshev_bounds!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.estimate_eigenvalue_bounds","page":"API","text":"estimate_eigenvalue_bounds(\n    op::Shakti.JacobiScaledOperator,\n    rhs::AbstractVector,\n    nsteps::Int64\n) -> Tuple{Any, Any}\n\n\nEstimates [lambda_min, lambda_max] of the Jacobi-scaled operator op (D^-1*A) via nsteps iterations of ordinary (unpreconditioned) CG, extracting the associated Lanczos tridiagonal matrix from the CG alpha/beta recurrence (Saad, section 6.7 – the same trick PETSc's KSPCHEBYSHEV uses) rather than running separate power-iteration machinery. Used by update_chebyshev_bounds! to size ChebyshevPreconditioner's polynomial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.estimate_eigenvalue_bounds"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AMGPreconditioner","page":"API","text":"The default, fastest-converging preconditioner for CGIterativeSolver{<:SparseAssembledLinearSystem}: Ruge-Stuben algebraic multigrid (AlgebraicMultigrid.jl), giving near mesh-independent CG iteration counts (measured ~flat 5-6 iterations from 64x64 to 256x256, vs. Jacobi's 192->745 and Chebyshev's 58->350 over the same grids – see test/benchmarks/). CPU/SparseMatrixCSC-only (AlgebraicMultigrid.jl has no GPU array support), so unlike ChebyshevPreconditioner this isn't offered for MatrixFreeLinearSystem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AMGPreconditioner"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.update_amg!","page":"API","text":"update_amg!(\n    P::AMGPreconditioner,\n    M::SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC\n) -> AMGPreconditioner\n\n\nRebuilds P's AMG hierarchy in place from M's current values (not free – see AMGPreconditioner's docstring for why this can't just be done once at construction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.update_amg!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractHeadRelaxation","page":"API","text":"Whether/how the raw Picard update to state.h is damped before the next iteration – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype (NoHeadRelaxation/UnderHeadRelaxation) picks whether relax_h! is a no-op or an under-relaxation blend with the previous iteration's head.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractHeadRelaxation"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.NoHeadRelaxation","page":"API","text":"No damping: each Picard iteration's raw linear-solve result is used as-is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.NoHeadRelaxation"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.UnderHeadRelaxation","page":"API","text":"Under-relaxation: blends the new head with the previous iteration's head by alpha (see relax_h!), trading slower convergence for extra stability on stiff problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.UnderHeadRelaxation"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.relax_h!","page":"API","text":"relax_h!(_::NoHeadRelaxation, state::State, h_prev) -> State\n\n\nNo-op under NoHeadRelaxation.\n\n\n\n\n\nrelax_h!(\n    hr::UnderHeadRelaxation,\n    state::State,\n    h_prev\n) -> State\n\n\nBlends state.h with h_prev by hr.alpha: h = alpha*h + (1-alpha)*h_prev. Applied before anything downstream of h is recomputed, so the next iteration's coefficients are consistent with the relaxed h.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.relax_h!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.PicardSolver","page":"API","text":"Drives the Picard iteration used to solve the nonlinear elliptic equation for hydraulic head: holds the linear solver (ls), optional head relaxation (hr), iteration/tolerance settings, and the scratch fields (h_prev, delta_h) the convergence check needs. Build one with the keyword-free constructor below; converged/last_iter are updated in place by Picard_loop! each time it's called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.PicardSolver"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.elliptic_solver!","page":"API","text":"elliptic_solver!(\n    ps::PicardSolver,\n    state::State,\n    grid::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    shs::AbstractSensibleHeatScheme,\n    kfs::AbstractKFaceScheme,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput,\n    sl::AbstractSlidingLaw\n)\n\n\nSolves the nonlinear elliptic equation for hydraulic head at the current timestep via Picard iteration (Picard_loop!), updating state and ps (ps.converged/ps.last_iter) in place.\n\nNotes\n\nstate/grid/p/shs are taken as separate arguments (rather than a bundled sim::Simulation) so this file doesn't need Simulation to already be defined – it can be included, and PicardSolver's struct fully written, before simulation.jl, letting EllipticHeadScheme{PS} (simulation.jl) use a proper PS <: PicardSolver bound instead of leaving PS unbounded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.elliptic_solver!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.Picard_loop!","page":"API","text":"Picard_loop!(\n    ps::PicardSolver,\n    state::State,\n    grid::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    shs::AbstractSensibleHeatScheme,\n    kfs::AbstractKFaceScheme,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput,\n    sl::AbstractSlidingLaw\n)\n\n\nRepeatedly calls Picard_iteration! (up to ps.iters times), checking convergence every ps.check_every iterations via a relative max-norm on the head update (max|delta_h| / (max|h| + eps) < ps.tol), and sets ps.converged/ps.last_iter accordingly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.Picard_loop!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.Picard_iteration!","page":"API","text":"Picard_iteration!(\n    ls::AbstractLinearSolver,\n    hr::AbstractHeadRelaxation,\n    s::State,\n    g::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    shs::AbstractSensibleHeatScheme,\n    kfs::AbstractKFaceScheme,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput,\n    sl::AbstractSlidingLaw,\n    h_prev\n) -> State\n\n\nOne Picard iteration: solves the linearized system for a new h (solve_linear_system!), optionally relaxes it (relax_h!), then refreshes every field that depends on the new h (pw, N, q/Re, taub, mdot, K) so the next iteration's linearization is consistent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.Picard_iteration!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractHeadScheme","page":"API","text":"How the hydraulic head is advanced each timestep – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype (see step_h! in run.jl) picks between EllipticHeadScheme (Picard iteration, used when p.e_v == 0) and ParabolicHeadScheme (not yet implemented).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractHeadScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.ParabolicHeadScheme","page":"API","text":"Head scheme for p.e_v != 0 (nonzero englacial storage void ratio) – not yet implemented, see run.jl's step_h!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.ParabolicHeadScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.EllipticHeadScheme","page":"API","text":"Head scheme used when p.e_v == 0: each timestep, ps (a PicardSolver) is run to convergence via Picard iteration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.EllipticHeadScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractGapScheme","page":"API","text":"How the gap height b is time-integrated each timestep – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype picks between ExplicitGapScheme and ImplicitGapScheme (see compute_b! in gap_height.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractGapScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.ExplicitGapScheme","page":"API","text":"Explicit (forward-Euler) update of b: cheaper per step, but only stable for small enough dt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.ExplicitGapScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.ImplicitGapScheme","page":"API","text":"Implicit (backward-Euler) update of b: unconditionally stable, the default choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.ImplicitGapScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.Simulation","page":"API","text":"Everything needed to run a subglacial hydrology simulation: the grid, state, model parameters, and every \"which scheme/law\" choice (head, gap, sensible-heat, K-face, melt-input, sliding-law) bundled together with the observer that records output. Build one with the keyword constructor below (not this positional one directly), then call run!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.Simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_H!","page":"API","text":"compute_H!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.H (ice thickness) from the current s.zs/s.zb/s.b: H = zs - (zb + b).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_H!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_po!","page":"API","text":"compute_po!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.po (ice overburden pressure) from the current s.H: po = rho_i*g*H.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_po!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_h!","page":"API","text":"compute_h!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.h (hydraulic head) from the current s.pw: h = pw/(rho_w*g) + zb.\n\nNotes\n\nOnly meant for seeding s.h from a prescribed initial s.pw during initial-condition setup. Once the time loop is running, h is instead the Picard solver's primary unknown, and pw is derived FROM h every iteration (compute_pw!), not the other way round.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_h!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_abs_ub!","page":"API","text":"compute_abs_ub!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.abs_ub (cell-centered sliding speed magnitude ‖v_b‖) by averaging s.ub_x/s.ub_y onto cell centers and taking the norm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_abs_ub!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_pw!","page":"API","text":"compute_pw!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.pw (water pressure) from the current s.h (hydraulic head): pw = rho_w*g*(h - zb).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_pw!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_N!","page":"API","text":"compute_N!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.N (effective pressure) from the current s.po/s.pw: N = po - pw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_N!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_dhdx!","page":"API","text":"compute_dhdx!(s::State, g::Grid) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.dhdx (x-gradient of hydraulic head) from the current s.h, zeroed on any face touching an OTHER_BASIN cell (s.valid_x) so downstream flux/melt computations never see a spurious gradient driven by a frozen, non-evolving value. Feeds compute_q_x!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_dhdx!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_dhdy!","page":"API","text":"compute_dhdy!(s::State, g::Grid) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.dhdy (y-gradient of hydraulic head), the y-face counterpart of compute_dhdx!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_dhdy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_dhdxy!","page":"API","text":"compute_dhdxy!(s::State, g::Grid) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_dhdx! + compute_dhdy!: one @parallel launch instead of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_dhdxy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_dpwdx!","page":"API","text":"compute_dpwdx!(s::State, g::Grid) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.dpwdx (x-gradient of water pressure) from the current s.pw, zeroed the same way as compute_dhdx!. Feeds the sensible-heat term in compute_mdot! (melt_rate.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_dpwdx!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_dpwdy!","page":"API","text":"compute_dpwdy!(s::State, g::Grid) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.dpwdy (y-gradient of water pressure), the y-face counterpart of compute_dpwdx!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_dpwdy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_dpwdxy!","page":"API","text":"compute_dpwdxy!(s::State, g::Grid) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_dpwdx! + compute_dpwdy!: one @parallel launch instead of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_dpwdxy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_q_x!","page":"API","text":"compute_q_x!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.q_x (x water flux) from the current s.b_x/s.dhdx/s.Re_x via the cubic (Poiseuille) flux law with a Darcy-Weisbach-style turbulence correction: q_x = -b_x^3*g*dhdx / (12*nu*(1+omega*Re_x)).\n\nNotes\n\nThis and compute_Re_x! are a LAGGED pair: q is computed from whatever Re the previous Picard iteration left behind, then Re is updated to match the new q – consistent with each other, but q is one iteration stale relative to the current dhdx/b. That lag can lock into a period-2 oscillation for large gradients instead of converging. The hot Picard path uses compute_q_and_Re_x! (exact, lag-free) instead; this pair is still used for the one-shot initial-condition seed, where a lagged initial guess is harmless.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_q_x!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_q_y!","page":"API","text":"compute_q_y!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.q_y (y water flux), the y-face counterpart of compute_q_x! (same lagged-pair caveat with compute_Re_y!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_q_y!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_q_xy!","page":"API","text":"compute_q_xy!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_q_x! + compute_q_y!: one @parallel launch instead of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_q_xy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_Re_x!","page":"API","text":"compute_Re_x!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.Re_x (x-face Reynolds number) from the current s.q_x: Re_x = |q_x|/nu. See compute_q_x! for the lagged-pair caveat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_Re_x!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_Re_y!","page":"API","text":"compute_Re_y!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.Re_y (y-face Reynolds number), the y-face counterpart of compute_Re_x!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_Re_y!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_Re_xy!","page":"API","text":"compute_Re_xy!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_Re_x! + compute_Re_y!: one @parallel launch instead of two.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_Re_xy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_Re!","page":"API","text":"compute_Re!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.Re (cell-centered Reynolds number) by averaging the four surrounding face values (s.Re_x/s.Re_y).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_Re!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_K!","page":"API","text":"compute_K!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.K (hydraulic transmissivity) from the current s.b/s.Re: the same cubic/ turbulence-corrected law as compute_q_x!/compute_q_y!, but expressed without the -dhdx/-dhdy factor, for use as the linear system's (off-diagonal) coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_K!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_q_and_Re_x!","page":"API","text":"compute_q_and_Re_x!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.q_x/s.Re_x together, exactly (no lag), from the current s.b_x/s.dhdx – the version used in the hot Picard path (see compute_q_x! for why the lagged pair isn't used there).\n\nNotes\n\nSubstituting Re = |q|/nu into the flux law (Eq. 5, Sommers et al. 2018) gives a quadratic in Re. Let D = b^3*g*|dhdx| / (12*nu^2); then omega*Re^2 + Re - D = 0, i.e. Re = (-1 + sqrt(1 + 4*omega*D)) / (2*omega), rearranged (multiply by the conjugate) to avoid catastrophic cancellation as omega -> 0 (exactly this model's regime, omega = 1e-4 to 1e-3): Re = 2*D / (1 + sqrt(1 + 4*omega*D)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_q_and_Re_x!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_q_and_Re_y!","page":"API","text":"compute_q_and_Re_y!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.q_y/s.Re_y together, exactly, the y-face counterpart of compute_q_and_Re_x!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_q_and_Re_y!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_q_and_Re_xy!","page":"API","text":"compute_q_and_Re_xy!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nFused version of compute_q_and_Re_x! + compute_q_and_Re_y!: one @parallel launch instead of two. This is the method actually called from the Picard loop (elliptic_solver.jl's Picard_iteration!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_q_and_Re_xy!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_beta!","page":"API","text":"compute_beta!(s::State, p::ModelParameters) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.beta (opening rate of the gap by sliding over bedrock bumps of height p.br and spacing p.lr, Röthlisberger-style cavity opening): positive only while the gap is still smaller than the bump height, zero once b has grown past br. p.br = 0 disables this opening mechanism entirely (beta is identically 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_beta!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_b_x!","page":"API","text":"compute_b_x!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.b_x by staggering s.b onto x-faces (boundary faces duplicate the nearest cell, interior faces average their two neighbours).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_b_x!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_b_y!","page":"API","text":"compute_b_y!(s::State) -> State\n\n\nUpdates s.b_y by staggering s.b onto y-faces (boundary faces duplicate the nearest cell, interior faces average their two neighbours).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_b_y!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.compute_b!","page":"API","text":"compute_b!(sim::Simulation) -> Simulation\n\n\nEvolves the gap height sim.state.b by one timestep, dispatching on sim.gs (ImplicitGapScheme()/ExplicitGapScheme()) to compute_b!(sim, sim.gs) below.\n\nNotes\n\nOnly evolves b where hydrology is actually being solved (GROUNDED). Cells with a Dirichlet-prescribed pw (LAND/OCEAN) or a frozen h (OTHER_BASIN) don't have a meaningfully-evolving b in this model, so their b is simply left untouched at whatever it was initialized to. Dispatching on sim.gs's type (rather than branching on a Symbol) means there's no error(\"Unknown scheme ...\") fallback to reach – the type system already guarantees sim.gs is one of the two schemes.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_b!(\n    sim::Simulation,\n    _::ImplicitGapScheme\n) -> Simulation\n\n\nImplicit (backward-Euler) update of sim.state.b: unconditionally stable, the scheme used by default.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompute_b!(\n    sim::Simulation,\n    _::ExplicitGapScheme\n) -> Simulation\n\n\nExplicit (forward-Euler) update of sim.state.b: cheaper per step, but only stable for small enough sim.dt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.compute_b!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.set_initial_conditions!","page":"API","text":"set_initial_conditions!(\n    s::State,\n    g::Grid,\n    p::ModelParameters,\n    mi::AbstractMeltInput,\n    sl::AbstractSlidingLaw,\n    mask,\n    A_visc,\n    zb,\n    zs,\n    b,\n    G,\n    ub_x,\n    ub_y,\n    ieb,\n    taub_x,\n    taub_y\n) -> State\n\n\nPopulates s (built with State(::Grid), fields all zero) with a real starting state: the raw input fields (mask, A_visc, zb, zs, b, G, ub_x, ub_y, ieb, taub_x, taub_y) are copied in (converting to s's backend/element type via Data.Array), then every derived field (H, beta, abs_ub, po, pw, N, h, gradients, Re, b_x/b_y, q_x/q_y, taub_x/taub_y, mdot, K) is computed from those in the same order the Picard loop itself would produce them, so s is immediately a valid state to time-step or Picard-solve from. pw is initialized to half of ice overburden pressure (po/2) as a generic starting guess – not a converged solution, see the elliptic solver for that – except where the mask calls for a prescribed Dirichlet value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.set_initial_conditions!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.save_checkpoint","page":"API","text":"save_checkpoint(path::String, sim::Simulation, t::Int64)\n\n\nSaves everything needed to resume sim's physics at timestep t: the full State (not just the observer's tracked_obs subset, which is often a small slice picked for output/analysis) and sim.total_time[].\n\nNotes\n\nWritten to a temp file and renamed into place (same filesystem, so mv is an atomic rename) rather than written directly to path, so a crash mid checkpoint-write can't leave a truncated/corrupt checkpoint behind – the previous good checkpoint at path stays intact until the new one has fully landed. See run.jl for how tsteps/checkpoint_every/restart_path tie together, and observer.jl's resume!/reopenfile! for resuming the tracked-output file itself alongside the physics state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.save_checkpoint"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.load_checkpoint!","page":"API","text":"load_checkpoint!(sim::Simulation, path::String) -> Any\n\n\nRestores sim.state and sim.total_time[] in place from a checkpoint written by save_checkpoint, and returns the timestep it was saved at, so run! knows where to resume the loop.\n\nNotes\n\nGeneric over State's fieldnames rather than a hardcoded field list, so it stays correct if State ever gains/loses fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.load_checkpoint!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.run!","page":"API","text":"run!(\n    sim::Simulation;\n    checkpoint_every,\n    checkpoint_path,\n    restart_path\n)\n\n\nRuns sim from t=0 (or from a checkpoint, see restart_path) up to sim.tsteps, calling step! each iteration and recording output via sim.observer.\n\nNotes\n\ncheckpoint_every/checkpoint_path: if both given, sim.state is saved to checkpoint_path (overwriting the previous checkpoint – only the latest is kept) every checkpoint_every tsteps, so a run killed at any point can be resumed via restart_path below instead of starting over from t=0.\n\nrestart_path: if given, resumes from a checkpoint written by a previous run! call instead of starting fresh at t=0 – loads sim.state/sim.total_time and continues the loop from the checkpointed tstep + 1 up to sim.tsteps (still the original total step count, not \"how many more steps to do\"). The observer's output file is reopened (not truncated) via resume! so it's appended to across the restart; see observer.jl for how each file writer handles a tstep that the crashed run may have already written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.run!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.step!","page":"API","text":"step!(sim::Simulation) -> State\n\n\nAdvances sim by one timestep: solves for the new head (step_h!) then evolves the gap height (step_b!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.step_h!","page":"API","text":"step_h!(hs::EllipticHeadScheme, sim::Simulation)\n\n\nSolves for the new hydraulic head under EllipticHeadScheme: updates sim.mi's melt input for the current time, then runs the Picard/elliptic solve.\n\n\n\n\n\nstep_h!(hs::ParabolicHeadScheme, sim::Simulation)\n\n\nNot yet implemented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.step_h!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.step_b!","page":"API","text":"step_b!(sim::Simulation) -> State\n\n\nEvolves the gap height sim.state.b for one timestep (compute_b!, dispatching internally on sim.gs), then refreshes everything that depends on it (beta, b_x, b_y) so they're ready for the next timestep's Picard loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.step_b!"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractFileWriter","page":"API","text":"Which file format an IOObserver writes to – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype (see openfile!/write2file!/reopenfile!/close_handle! below) picks NetCDFFileWriter, HDF5FileWriter, JLD2FileWriter, or CSVFileWriter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractFileWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.NetCDFFileWriter","page":"API","text":"Records the full tracked fields at each tracked time, one dataset per field (shape field's own shape..., ntimes), written one time-slice at a time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.NetCDFFileWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.HDF5FileWriter","page":"API","text":"Same recording scheme as NetCDFFileWriter, backed by HDF5 instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.HDF5FileWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.JLD2FileWriter","page":"API","text":"Same recording scheme as NetCDFFileWriter, backed by JLD2 instead (no preallocated shape – each tracked time is its own key).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.JLD2FileWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.CSVFileWriter","page":"API","text":"Records one row per tracked time, with min/max/mean columns per tracked field – unlike the other writers, meant for scalar summaries (e.g. domain-averaged melt rate over time), not full grids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.CSVFileWriter"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.AbstractObserver","page":"API","text":"How simulation output is recorded each timestep – multiple dispatch on the concrete subtype picks NoObserver (nothing recorded), LiveObserver (kept in RAM), or IOObserver (written to disk). See prepare!/observe!/ finalize!, called from run! (run.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.AbstractObserver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.NoObserver","page":"API","text":"Records nothing. Used when tracked_obs is empty (see Simulation's constructor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.NoObserver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.IOObserver","page":"API","text":"Writes tracked_obs (names of State fields) at each of tracked_times (timestep indices) to path, in the file format given by fr. handle is set by prepare!/resume! and holds the open file/dataset handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.IOObserver"},{"category":"type","location":"API/#Shakti.LiveObserver","page":"API","text":"Keeps tracked_obs (names of State fields) at each of tracked_times (timestep indices) in memory (history, one preallocated array per tracked observable) rather than writing to disk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.LiveObserver"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.get_observable","page":"API","text":"get_observable(state::State, name::String) -> AbstractArray\n\n\nResolves a tracked observable's name (as given by the user in tracked_obs) to the actual State field, e.g. get_observable(state, \"h\") -> state.h. Validity of name (i.e. that it's really a State field) is checked once, at Simulation-construction time (see simulation.jl), not on every call here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.get_observable"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.prepare!","page":"API","text":"prepare!(observer::NoObserver, state::State)\n\n\nSets up observer before the time loop starts – a no-op for NoObserver; preallocates history for LiveObserver; opens the output file for IOObserver (see openfile!, dispatched on observer.fr).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.prepare!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.observe!","page":"API","text":"observe!(observer::NoObserver, state::State, t, total_time)\n\n\nRecords state at timestep t (elapsed time total_time) if t is one of observer's tracked times – a no-op for NoObserver; appends into history for LiveObserver; writes to disk for IOObserver (see write2file!, dispatched on observer.fr). Called once per timestep from run! (run.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.observe!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.openfile!","page":"API","text":"openfile!(\n    fr::NetCDFFileWriter,\n    observer::IOObserver,\n    state::State\n) -> NetCDF.NcFile\n\n\nCreates and opens observer.path for writing under observer.fr's format, sized to hold every tracked field at every tracked time. Called once by prepare!; returns the handle stored in observer.handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.openfile!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.write2file!","page":"API","text":"write2file!(\n    fr::NetCDFFileWriter,\n    observer::IOObserver,\n    state::State,\n    idx::Int64,\n    total_time::AbstractFloat\n)\n\n\nWrites state's tracked fields (and total_time) into observer's open file at time-slice idx. Called once per tracked timestep by observe!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.write2file!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.finalize!","page":"API","text":"finalize!(observer::AbstractObserver, state::State)\n\n\nTears down observer after the time loop finishes – a no-op for NoObserver/ LiveObserver (nothing to close); closes the output file/handle for IOObserver. Called once from run! (run.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.finalize!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.resume!","page":"API","text":"resume!(observer::NoObserver, state::State, resume_t::Int64)\n\n\nSets up observer when resuming from a checkpoint at resume_t (see checkpoint.jl/run.jl), instead of prepare!: a no-op for NoObserver (nothing to resume); LiveObserver just starts a fresh (gap-containing) buffer via prepare!, since its history lived only in the crashed process's RAM and can't be recovered; IOObserver reopens its existing output file for further writes instead of truncating it (see reopenfile!), so the resumed run continues writing into the same file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.resume!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.reopenfile!","page":"API","text":"reopenfile!(\n    fr::NetCDFFileWriter,\n    observer::IOObserver,\n    state::State,\n    resume_t::Int64\n) -> NetCDF.NcFile\n\n\nReopens observer.path for further writes when resuming from a checkpoint at resume_t, instead of truncating it via openfile!. Called once by resume!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.reopenfile!"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.make_mp4_mid","page":"API","text":"make_mp4_mid(\n    hist::AbstractArray,\n    tracked_times,\n    j,\n    moulin_ij;\n    filename,\n    show_moulins\n)\n\n\nRenders an animated .mp4 of a 1D slice (row j) of a tracked field's time history hist (shape field's own shape..., ntimes, e.g. from LiveObserver's history[name]) to filename, one frame per tracked time, labeled by the real timestep number (tracked_times). moulin_ij (a vector of (i,j) grid indices, see get_moulin_ij) optionally overlays moulin locations on row j as red markers. Uses CairoMakie's software rasterizer, so this works on headless HPC nodes as well as locally.\n\n\n\n\n\nmake_mp4_mid(\n    obs::LiveObserver,\n    name::String,\n    j,\n    moulin_ij;\n    filename,\n    show_moulins\n)\n\n\nConvenience wrapper: make_mp4_mid directly off a LiveObserver's named tracked field, instead of manually unpacking obs.history[name]/obs.tracked_times.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.make_mp4_mid"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.make_mp4_2d","page":"API","text":"make_mp4_2d(\n    hist::AbstractArray,\n    tracked_times,\n    moulin_ij;\n    filename,\n    show_moulins\n)\n\n\nRenders an animated .mp4 heatmap of a tracked field's full 2D time history hist, the 2D counterpart of make_mp4_mid above (same arguments otherwise).\n\n\n\n\n\nmake_mp4_2d(\n    obs::LiveObserver,\n    name::String,\n    moulin_ij;\n    filename,\n    show_moulins\n)\n\n\nConvenience wrapper: make_mp4_2d directly off a LiveObserver's named tracked field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.make_mp4_2d"},{"category":"function","location":"API/#Shakti.get_moulin_ij","page":"API","text":"get_moulin_ij(state::State) -> Any\n\n\nExtracts (i,j) moulin locations from state.ieb's nonzero entries, so you don't have to hand-build moulin_ij yourself for make_mp4_mid/make_mp4_2d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Shakti.get_moulin_ij"}]
}
